(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-09"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Series and parallel rules from Class 11 cannot solve every circuit. This chapter gives general rules (Kirchhoff's laws) and the instruments built on them: the Wheatstone bridge, the potentiometer and the galvanometer.","w":["Kirchhoff's laws work for any network; series and parallel rules are only shortcuts for simple ones."]},"9.2":{"s":"A junction is a point where conductors meet; a loop is any closed path. Kirchhoff's current law: the total current into a junction equals the total current out (charge is conserved). Kirchhoff's voltage law: around any closed loop, the sum of emfs equals the sum of IR drops (energy is conserved). Take careful sign conventions.","f":[{"x":"Sigma I = 0","n":"Current law at a junction: currents in taken positive, currents out negative (charge is conserved).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.2, p.214"},{"x":"Sigma I*R + Sigma E = 0","n":"Voltage law around any closed loop: the IR drops and emfs, with signs, add to zero (energy is conserved).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.2, p.215"}],"w":["Pick a direction for each current; a negative answer only means the real current flows the other way.","Going across a cell from − to + adds its emf; going along the current through a resistor drops IR."]},"9.3":{"s":"A Wheatstone bridge is four resistances P, Q, R and S joined in a loop, with a cell across one diagonal and a galvanometer across the other. It is balanced (no galvanometer current) when the ratio of two neighbouring arms equals the ratio of the other two; in the book's figure this is P/Q = S/R. So one unknown resistance follows from three known ones. The metre bridge uses this idea.","f":[{"x":"P/Q = S/R","n":"Balance condition (galvanometer current zero) for the bridge labelled as in the book's figure.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.3, p.217"},{"x":"X = R*l_X/l_R","n":"Metre bridge: unknown X in the left gap, known R in the right; l_X and l_R = balancing lengths of the wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.3, p.218"}],"w":["Balance does not depend on the cell's emf or the galvanometer's resistance.","Metre bridge: the balance point should be near the middle of the wire for the smallest error."]},"9.4":{"s":"A potentiometer measures potential difference without drawing current from the source, so it reads the true emf. It is a long uniform wire carrying a steady current. The fall of potential per unit length is constant, so the balancing length is proportional to the voltage. Uses: comparing emfs, finding a cell's internal resistance. A longer wire gives better sensitivity.","f":[{"x":"K = V_AB/L","n":"Potential gradient: fall of potential per unit length of the uniform potentiometer wire (V/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.221"},{"x":"V = K*l","n":"Potential difference across a length l of the wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.221"},{"x":"E_1/E_2 = l_1/l_2","n":"Comparing two emfs: l_1, l_2 = balancing lengths for each cell.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.222"},{"x":"E_1/E_2 = (l_1 + l_2)/(l_1 - l_2)","n":"Sum and difference method: l_1 with the cells adding, l_2 with them opposing.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.223"},{"x":"r = R*(l_1/l_2 - 1)","n":"Internal resistance of a cell: l_1 without, l_2 with the resistance box R across the cell.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.223"}],"w":["At balance no current is drawn from the cell being tested, so the potentiometer reads its emf, not its terminal voltage.","If no null point is found, the driving cell's emf is smaller than the emf being measured (or a cell is joined the wrong way)."]},"9.5":{"s":"A moving-coil galvanometer detects small currents: the coil turns by an amount proportional to the current. A low resistance (shunt) in parallel turns it into an ammeter. A high resistance in series turns it into a voltmeter.","f":[{"x":"S = G*I_g/(I - I_g)","n":"Shunt (in parallel) that turns a galvanometer of resistance G and full-scale current I_g into an ammeter of range I.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.225"},{"x":"S = G/(n - 1)","n":"Shunt to raise the current range n times (I = n*I_g).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.225"},{"x":"X = V/I_g - G","n":"Series resistance that turns the galvanometer into a voltmeter of range V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.226"}],"w":["Ammeter: small shunt in PARALLEL (low total resistance). Voltmeter: large resistance in SERIES (high total resistance).","Convert micro- and milliamperes to amperes before using the formulas."]}};
